theorem:
\begin{theorem}[Count preservation]\label{thm:inv}
If $|x'-x|\le\varepsilon$ and every $T\in T_\bullet$ is buffered as above, then $C(x';T_\bullet)=C(x;T_\bullet)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
If $T-\varepsilon\le x<T+\varepsilon$, there exists $|x'-x|\le\varepsilon$ with $C(x';[T])\neq C(x;[T])$.
\end{theorem}

examples:
\begin{example}
$T=(1,3,5)$, $x=4$, $\varepsilon=0.2$: passes $T=1,3$ so $C=2$; buffered cuts keep $C=2$. If $T=3$, $x=3.1$, $\varepsilon=0.2$, then $x'=x-\varepsilon$ can drop the pass bit.
\end{example}

proof:
\paragraph{Preservation.}
Induct on the threshold list. The empty list has count $0$. For $T{::}\mathrm{tail}$, allCoordsStable gives a scalar buffer on $T$, so threshold preservation yields $\mathrm{passBit}(x',T)=\mathrm{passBit}(x,T)$. The inductive hypothesis equates the tail counts. Adding proves Theorem~\ref{thm:inv}.

\paragraph{Sharpness.}
Assume $T-\varepsilon\le x<T+\varepsilon$. If $x\ge T$, take $x'=x-\varepsilon$ (bit drops). If $x<T$, take $x'=x+\varepsilon$ (bit rises). This proves Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.

